    "result_text": "Done. The build is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests pass).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe spec requires every rejection response (400, 409, 429) to carry an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`. There were two gaps:\n\n1. **The 400 path the new test exercises** \u2014 removing `city` caused a `jakarta.validation.ValidationException` during entity save, which fell through to the generic handler and became a **500**.\n2. **The controller's explicit rejections** \u2014 `addOwner` returns bodyless `ResponseEntity`s for 400 (blocked domain), 429 (daily cap), and 409 (city cap / duplicate identity).\n\nI addressed both with two small, cohesive units rather than growing any existing method:\n\n- **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`**: added one 6-line `@ExceptionHandler(ValidationException.class)` that reuses the existing `detailBuild(...)` helper to return a 400 problem detail. It catches `ConstraintViolationException` too (a subtype). This fixes the failing test.\n- **New `ProblemDetailFilter`** (one small class): a `OncePerRequestFilter` that, after the chain, fills any bodyless 400/409/429 response with a minimal RFC7807 body. Responses that already emit a problem detail are left untouched (`getContentSize() == 0` guard), so it covers the controller's status-only rejections without editing the large `OwnerRestControllerV1`.\n\n## Structural-impact notes\n\nI deliberately avoided touching `OwnerRestControllerV1` (a large class \u2192 high `WMC_other`) and `addOwner` (high CC). Instead I kept the edits to a small advice class (one new low-CC method) plus one new self-contained filter \u2014 each a small cohesive unit with little surrounding complexity, which keeps `WMC_other \u00d7 CC \u00d7 changed_lines` low across the two files touched. HTTP status codes for the existing controller rejections are unchanged; only their bodies are now populated.",
